{"id": "524bbdfd-d706-48b1-84a5-25cda942ca66", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T16:51:43.854111", "source": "user", "parent_id": "2009db8c-5b71-4897-afe5-ff3366f33ae5", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"This is a design-only task: produce an experiment design (no execution required) that addresses how to identify rare patterns in a tabular dataset using Docta. I will not run a pipeline; I will only inspect the input/repository, propose an approach, and flag assumptions and uncertainties for me (the researcher) to acknowledge. Please respond with the following three sections only — do not produce the full experiment write-up or claim any run has occurred. (1) Input/repository inspection — list the files in /workspace and any content under track.md (quote its text). If a local Docta package, docs, or example notebooks are present, describe their layout and quote any relevant API surface (e.g., classes/functions named for pattern mining, rare-item mining, contrast mining, anomaly/rule discovery). Do not fabricate features; only report what is visible. (2) Proposed approach (design, not execution) — outline, in prose with bullets: (a) how 'rare pattern' is operationalized here (support/frequency threshold, rarity via low marginal frequency vs. surprising conditional co-occurrence, e.g., leverage/lift/J-measure), (b) candidate Docta primitives that could be reused (only those actually visible in the repo/docs), (c) data preparation pipeline definition, (d) baseline comparators that would be included (e.g., pointwise anomaly scoring such as isolation forest; rule-based mining such as Apriori/FP-Growth with very low minsup; rare-class rare-pattern mining such as contrast/emerging pattern miners, only if visible), (e) controls (null/randomized-feature data, downsampled rare-class ratios, knowledge-leak-free splits, fixed random seeds). (f) evaluation plan: metrics per pattern family (support, confidence, lift, leverage, recovaboratory metrics such as precision@k of recovered known-rare templates if synthetic ground truth is injected, runtime, stability across seeds), and (g) feasibility given bubblewrap sandbox, disabled network, no preinstalled project dependencies, Python 3 only. (3) Assumptions and uncertainties — enumerate every setup detail that the researcher has not explicitly supplied (definition of 'rare', dataset(s) to use, target column/churn/label-free, runtime budget, software environment outside stdlib, scoring window, whether Docta must be installed via local source or treated as an external reference, etc.) and tag each as 'assumed', 'needs confirmation', or 'cannot be derived from supplied docs'. Do not request experiments to be run; do not estimate final numerical results; do not grade your own work. Output in plain Markdown, in the researcher-facing tone used at initial_inspection.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"How can you identify rare patterns in a tabular dataset using Docta?\", \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\", \"environment\": {\"runtime\": \"bubblewrap\", \"network\": \"disabled\", \"repository\": \"/workspace\", \"python\": \"python3\", \"software_environment\": \"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\", \"datasets\": [], \"gpu_enabled\": false, \"cpu_affinity_count\": 24, \"note\": \"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\"}, \"researcher_topics\": [], \"question_budget\": 6, \"actor_step_budget\": 60, \"continue_after_initial_plan\": false}}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 1}
