ta source/docs are visible, no Docta class or function is named in 2(b); the harness is the reusable layer.", "assumptions": [], "uncertainties": [], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-139c1bb7-a653-4a04-9be7-371b6346dc2f", "tool_call": {"id": "call-139c1bb7-a653-4a04-9be7-371b6346dc2f", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Propose initial rare-pattern experiment design\", \"stage\": \"plan\", \"answer\": \"## 1) Input / repository inspection\\n\\n- **`/workspace` listing** (verbatim):\\n  - `drwx------  1776737281 ... 4096 Oct 21 23:51 .`\\n  - `drwx-xr-x  1776737281 ...  240 Oct 21 23:51 ..`\\n  - `-rw-------  1776737281 ...  363 Oct 21 23:51 task.json`\\n  - Sole file present: `/workspace/task.json` (363 bytes). No `track.md`, no `/src`, no `README`, no `pyproject.toml`, no `notebooks/`, no `data/`.\\n\\n- **`track.md` content** \\u2014 there is **no `track.md` file**. The only reference to it is inside `task.json` under `documents.track.md`, which I quote verbatim from that file:\\n  > `\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"`\\n\\n- **Docta package / docs / examples** \\u2014 none present:\\n  - `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname \\\"docta*\\\"` returns nothing.\\n  - `python3 -c \\\"import docta\\\"` \\u2192 `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'docta'`.\\n  - No `docs/`, no `examples/`, no notebook files, no API surface visible. Because the prompt explicitly forbids fabricating features, I will not name any Docta class, function, or signature in section 2 \\u2014 there is nothing here to quote.\\n\\n- **Runtime / environment probe**:\\n  - Python: `3.12.3`.\\n  - Network: disabled (per `task.json`'s `environment.network=\\\"disabled\\\"`, consistent with `find` finishing quickly and no `/root` mail/cache for fetches).\\n  - Preinstalled project-relevant packages absent (pandas, numpy, scikit-learn, scipy, mlxtend all missing).\\n  - Per `task.json`: `software_environment = \\\"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\\\"`, `datasets = []`, GPU off, 24 CPUs, `bubblewrap` runtime.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2) Proposed approach (design only \\u2014 not executed)\\n\\n### (a) Operationalization of \\\"rare pattern\\\"\\nBecause \\\"rare\\\" is ambiguous and the task gives no dataset or label, the design must allow **three co-existing definitions**, applied and reported separately:\\n\\n- **R1 \\u2014 Low marginal support.** A feature-value combination (itemset) whose absolute support falls below a pre-specified threshold `minsup_abs` (e.g. \\u2264 0.5% of rows, but \\u2265 5 rows to suppress noise). Operationalized via classic itemset counting over a discretized / one-hot matrix.\\n- **R2 \\u2014 Low support yet surprising co-occurrence.** Itemsets whose joint frequency is *much lower* than what independence would predict. Quantify with **support, confidence, lift, leverage, and the J-measure** between every pair, and between an antecedent set and a target item; flag those whose `lift` deviates strongly from 1 in either direction and whose absolute support is small. This is what \\\"rare but informative\\\" usually means in contrast-mining literature.\\n- **R3 \\u2014 Rare *class*-conditional.** If a target column is supplied, restrict mining to a rare class value (the rarer target bucket) and look for itemsets that are over-represented there vs. elsewhere \\u2014 i.e. the *emerging-pattern / contrast-pattern* framing, plus class-conditional lift.\\n\\nThe design will report each R1/R2/R3 finding under its own header so they can be evaluated independently.\\n\\n### (b) Docta primitives that could be reused\\n- **None visible in the supplied repository.** No Docta source, docstrings, exports, or examples exist in `/workspace` or in any system path I could inspect. Therefore I will *not* list candidate primitives by name; any \\\"Dear Docta component for X\\\" claim would be fabricated. The reusable layer is **purely the data-prep + evaluation harness I describe below**, written to one stable interface (e.g. a `RarePatternMiner` protocol with `fit(df) -> RarePatternResult`). When/if a Docta checkout is provided, the design says: *\\\"wrap whatever pattern/discovery class it exposes so that it satisfies `RarePatternMiner`\\\"* \\u2014 the harness is what is reusable, not any specific Docta API that is not in the repo.\\n\\n### (c) Data preparation pipeline (defined, not run)\\n1. **Load** tabular data from a path supplied at runtime (format by extension; CSV assumed if unspecified).\\n2. **Schema audit**: column dtypes, cardinality per categorical, missingness rate, row count.\\n3. **Type treatment**:\\n   - Numeric \\u2192 optionally **discretize** into quantile bins (default `n_bins=10`), so every column is itemset-friendly; raw numeric summaries retained alongside.\\n   - Categorical / boolean \\u2192 passthrough with NaN bucketed as `_NA`.\\n   - High-cardinality string columns (> 50 uniques) \\u2192 either top-K + `_OTHER` (e.g. K=20) or hash-bucketed (e.g. mod 32). Choice recorded as a config knob.\\n4. **One-hot / item encoding** of the discretized matrix into a boolean item matrix.\\n5. **Splitting**: a **knowledge-leak-free** train/test split (default 70/30, stratified on the target column when one exists); split indices saved. The mining step runs on `train` only; metrics are reported on `test` to characterize generalization.\\n6. **Rare-class subsampling knob** (for R3): ability to **downsample** a rare class to a configurable ratio (e.g. 1%, 5%) to probe sensitivity.\\n\\n### (d) Baseline comparators\\nThe design includes three families. *Whether each is actually runnable in the sandbox is answered in (g); here is what is in-scope if the deps were present.*\\n\\n- **Pointwise anomaly scoring** (defines a *per-row* notion of rarity; complementary to itemset rarity):\\n  - Isolation Forest on numeric+dummy features.\\n  - Local Outlier Factor (unsupervised).\\n  - Mahalanobis distance on numeric features after standard scaling.\\n- **Classical rule mining at very low minsup** (provides R1 / partial R2):\\n  - Apriori, with `minsup` swept (e.g. {0.001, 0.005, 0.01}) and `minconf` swept (e.g. {0.5, 0.8}).\\n  - FP-Growth as a runtime sanity check.\\n  - Filter downstream rules to those meeting a lift criterion (e.g. `lift \\u2265 1.5` or `lift \\u2264 0.5`) and a J-measure ranking.\\n- **Contrast / rare-class mining** (R3 only, if a target is supplied):\\n  - Emerging-pattern-style growth: enumerate itemsets, score by growth rate `supp_class / supp_other`, report top-K by growth rate subject to a minimum support floor in the rare class.\\n  - Optionally a contrast-set miner (e.g. STUCCO-style) if a target column exists.\\n\\n### (e) Controls\\n- **Random-feature null**: append K synthetic columns drawn uniformly from a permuted copy of an existing column. A correct rare-pattern procedure should produce near-zero lift / leverage on these; report this rate as a type-I control.\\n- **Rare-class ratio sweep**: re-run R3 at injected rare-class ratios of {1%, 5%, 10%, 20%} to test sensitivity.\\n- **Discretization-bin sweep**: quantile bins \\u2208 {5, 10, 20} to test sensitivity to encoding.\\n- **Seeds**: fixed seeds (`seed \\u2208 {0,1,2,3,4}`) for any stochastic step; results aggregated as mean \\u00b1 std; the seed is set **before** sampling and **before** model fitting in every cell of the sweep.\\n- **Knowledge-leak-free split**: as in (c); no fit-step sees test rows.\\n- **Itemset-size cap**: report results per `max_itemset_size \\u2208 {2, 3, 4}` so combinatorics stay bounded and behavior at higher widths is honestly characterized.\\n- **Runtime budget per cell** with a hard cap and a \\\"timed-out\\\" flag preserved in the result table.\\n\\n### (f) Evaluation plan\\nReported per pattern family and per `R1/R2/R3` definition:\\n\\n- **Pattern-level statistics**: number of itemsets emitted, distribution of support, distribution of `lift`, distribution of `leverage`, distribution of `J-measure`, distribution of `confidence`.\\n- **Recovery of injected ground truth** (synthetic-injection evaluation): build a held-out synthetic dataset in which *known rare templates* (e.g. \\\"column A=high AND column B=low\\\", with controllable support \\u2248 0.5\\u20132%) are planted. Report **precision@k, recall@k, F1@k** for k \\u2208 {10, 50, 200} of the top-k returned patterns vs. the planted templates. This is the cleanest evaluation because the natural dataset has no labelled rare pattern ground truth.\\n- **Null-control rate**: fraction of synthetic random-feature columns that produce a \\\"rare pattern\\\" hit; should be \\u2248 0.\\n- **Stability**: Jaccard overlap of the top-K returned sets across the seed sweep.\\n- **Cross-split generalization**: support / lift / confidence of patterns found in train, recomputed on test; report median relative change.\\n- **Rare-class recovery** (R3 only): PR-AUC of (mined rare-class emerging patterns ranked by growth rate) against the rare-class labels on test.\\n- **Runtime**: wall-clock per pass, per cell of all sweeps, plus total.\\n\\n### (g) Feasibility under the bubblewrap sandbox\\nThe sandbox note in `task.json` says **the task is design-only**, but `runtime=\\\"bubblewrap\\\"`, `network=\\\"disabled\\\"`, project deps not preinstalled, and the only file in `/workspace` is `task.json` with no data. The honest feasibility answer:\\n\\n- **Pure-stdlib step (always runnable here)**: schema audit, one-hot encoding, support/lift/leverage/J-measure on already-encoded items, null-control with random permutations, seed handling, metric aggregation \\u2014 all stdlib (`csv`, `collections`, `itertools`, `statistics`, `math`, `random`).\\n- **Deps that are not preinstalled and cannot be fetched (network disabled)**: pandas, numpy, scikit-learn, mlxtend (Apriori / FP-Growth), scipy. The design assumes they would be present for the *full* pipeline; if they are not, the design degrades gracefully to the stdlib subset above, which still covers R1 and most of the diagnostics in (f).\\n- **No Docta available** \\u2014 see (b): there is no Docta source to import, and the network is off, so `pip install docta` is not available. If Docta must be exercised, the researcher must supply a local checkout or pre-install it; otherwise the design treats Docta as an external reference and the harness wraps whichever class the researcher names.\\n- **GPU off, 24 CPUs** \\u2014 Apriori / FP-Growth at very low minsup can explode combinatorially; the `max_itemset_size` cap and minsup floor are deliberate safeguards to keep runtime sane on 24 CPUs.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3) Assumptions and uncertainties\\n\\nTagged honestly as: **`assumed`** (I am proceeding under this), **`needs confirmation`** (please confirm before I expand), **`cannot be derived from supplied docs`** (no source to read).\\n\\n1. **Definition of \\\"rare\\\"** \\u2014 *needs confirmation*. I have defaulted to the three-tier R1/R2/R3 described in 2(a).\\n2. **Dataset(s) to use** \\u2014 *needs confirmation*. `task.json::environment.datasets = []`; no data file is visible in `/workspace`.\\n3. **Label / target column** \\u2014 *needs confirmation*. R3 is conditional on this. Default assumption if a target exists: rare class = the smallest-frequency level with \\u2265 1 row on both splits.\\n4. **Whether Docta must be installed and exercised** \\u2014 *needs confirmation*. The supplied repository contains no Docta artifacts, so I cannot quote its API.\\n5. **Origin of Docta (local source vs. external reference)** \\u2014 *needs confirmation*. With network disabled, no install is possible from inside the sandbox.\\n6. **Runtime budget** \\u2014 *needs confirmation*. `actor_step_budget=60`, `question_budget=6` are conversation budgets; there is no numeric compute-time budget given for one experiment cell.\\n7. **Discretization choice and bin count for numeric columns*