on@k, recall@k, F1@k для k∈{10,50,200} с planted templates 0.5–2% support; null-control rate; seed-stability via Jaccard на top-K; train→test support drift; PR-AUC для R3; runtime per cell + total; (8) Feasibility — stdlib subset всегда работоспособен (csv, collections, itertools, statistics, math, random), degraded mode без pandas/numpy/sklearn/mlxtend; Docta отсутствует в репозитории и network disabled → pipeline проектируется как Docta-agnostic, harness wraps whatever class the researcher names; GPU off, 24 CPUs, minsup floors и size cap как защита от комбинаторного взрыва; (9) Assumptions — таблица assumption/default/source для всех defaults из пункта 3; (10) Limitations — прозрачно: нет Docta в поставке, dataset пуст в task.json, ground truth только synthetic-injection, no execution claimed. Никаких таблиц с числами, никакого claim 'эксперимент проведён', никакого кода кроме коротких псевдо-сигнатур интерфейса. Жду финальный документ одним ответом.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 18}
{"id": "c654a366-78cc-4fe6-aee0-ac2ff8f33c5b", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T16:55:44.191647", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "34a33ea9-47ef-45eb-a4aa-275939a44dde", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Identifying Rare Patterns in Tabular Data with Docta — Experiment Design (Design-Only)\n\n## 1. Title & TL;DR\n\n**Title.** A Docta-agnostic, plug-in experimental harness for surfacing rare patterns in tabular data, with three operational definitions of \"rare\" (R1 low marginal support, R2 rare-but-surprising co-occurrence, R3 rare-class-conditional), three baseline families (pointwise anomaly, low-minsup itemset, contrast/emerging pattern), and explicit synthetic-injection evaluation because no labelled rare-pattern ground truth is supplied.\n\n**TL;DR.** We design — but do not execute — an experiment that (i) operationalises \"rare pattern\" along three independent axes, (ii) ingests any tabular dataset and any \"miner\" object via a thin `RarePatternMiner` protocol so a future Docta class drops in without harness changes, (iii) compares the miner against stdlib-aware baselines under strict knowledge-leak-free controls and seed sweeps, and (iv) reports pattern-level statistics, synthetic-injection precision/recall/F1@k, null-control rate, seed stability (Jaccard on top-K), train-to-test support drift, rare-class PR-AUC, and runtime. Because the supplied repository contains no Docta artifacts and has no network, the design is Docta-agnostic: it captures whatever class the researcher later names instead of fabricating an API.\n\n## 2. Research question (restated)\n\n> \"How can you identify rare patterns in a tabular dataset using Docta?\"\n\nDecomposed into answerable design questions:\n\n- **Q1.** How is \"rare\" operationalised across the R1 / R2 / R3 axes, and which thresholds, formulas, and bounds apply?\n- **Q2.** What data preparation is needed so that a generic rare-pattern procedure (including any Docta-provided one) can run reproducibly on a tabular input?\n- **Q3.** Which baselines form a fair comparison group, and which metrics separate signal from noise on an *unlabelled* rare-pattern task?\n- **Q4.** What controls and feasibility constraints govern the experiment (nulls, sweeps, leak-free splits, seeds, runtime, sandbox)?\n\n## 3. Operationalisation of \"rare\" — three levels\n\nLet `X`, `Y` denote itemsets over the discretised/one-hot matrix; `N` the row count in the mining split (train); `c` a class label. All frequencies are estimated on the **training split only**.\n\n### 3.1 R1 — Low marginal support\n\nA singleton or itemset whose absolute empirical support falls below a small floor and is therefore individually uncommon.\n\n- `support(X) = |{i : X ⊆ row_i}| / N`\n- Inclusion criterion: `support(X) ≤ minsup`  with `N · support(X) ≥ min_count` (≥ 5 rows by default) to suppress single-row noise.\n\n**Default `minsup` sweep:** `{0.001, 0.005, 0.01}` (≈ 0.1%, 0.5%, 1.0% of rows). For each `minsup`, R1 emits every qualifying itemset up to `max_itemset_size ∈ {2, 3, 4}`.\n\n### 3.2 R2 — Rare-but-surprising co-occurrence\n\nItemsets whose joint frequency deviates strongly from independence, *and* whose absolute support remains small. We score every qualifying rule `X ⇒ y` (single consequent) using four classical quantities plus J-measure:\n\n- `confidence(X→y) = support(X ∪ {y}) / support(X)`\n- `lift(X→y) = support(X ∪ {y}) / (support(X) · support({y}))`\n- `leverage(X→y) = support(X ∪ {y}) − support(X) · support({y})`\n- `J-measure(X; y) = P(X,y) · log2(P(X,y) / (P(X)·P(y))) + P(X,¬y) · log2(P(X,¬y) / (P(X)·P(¬y)))`\n\n**Inclusion criterion (R2).** `support(X ∪ {y}) ≤ minsup_R2` (use the same `minsup` sweep as R1 by default), **and** `(lift ≤ lift_lo  OR  lift ≥ lift_hi)` with default `lift_lo=0.5`, `lift_hi=1.5`, **and** the rule is ranked primarily by `J-measure` with `leverage` and `lift` as tie-breakers.\n\n### 3.3 R3 — Rare-class-conditional / emerging patterns\n\nRestrict mining to the rare class bucket and look for itemsets that are over-represented there vs. the rest. The headline score is growth rate:\n\n- `growth_rate(X; c) = support_c(X) / support_¬c(X)`            (with smoothing `support_c(X)+ε` / `support_¬c(X)+ε`, ε = `1/(2N)`)\n- `support_diff(X; c) = support_c(X) − support_¬c(X)`\n- `class_lift(X; c) = support_c(X) / [ P(c) · support(X) ]`     (factor by which `X` is enriched in class `c`)\n\n**Inclusion criterion (R3).** `support_c(X) ≥ min_class_sup` (≥ 5 rows in the rare class), `growth_rate(X; c) ≥ GR_min` (default `GR_min ∈ {2, 5, 10}`), reported per `max_itemset_size ∈ {2, 3, 4}`.\n\n**Rare-class ratio sweep:** `{1%, 5%, 10%, 20%}` of rows held by the rare class, induced by downsampling the rare class (or by class-stratified subsampling when the natural dataset has none). This sweep probes robustness of R3 to class imbalance.\n\n### 3.4 Defaults summary (locked for this design)\n\n| Knob                   | Default                                                  |\n|------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|\n| `minsup` sweep         | `{0.001, 0.005, 0.01}`                                   |\n| `min_count`            | `5` rows                                                 |\n| `max_itemset_size`     | `{2, 3, 4}`                                              |\n| `lift` thresholds      | `lift_lo=0.5`, `lift_hi=1.5`                             |\n| `J-measure`            | primary R2 ranking; `leverage`, `lift` as tie-breakers   |\n| Rare-class ratio sweep | `{0.01, 0.05, 0.10, 0.20}`                               |\n| Growth-rate threshold  | `GR_min ∈ {2, 5, 10}`                                    |\n| Quantile bins          | `{5, 10, 20}`                                            |\n| High-cardinality rule  | top-K=20 levels + `_OTHER` bucket                        |\n| Split                  | 70/30 train/test, **stratified by target** when present  |\n| Seeds                  | `{0, 1, 2, 3, 4}`                                        |\n| Runtime cap per cell   | enforced with a `TIMEOUT` flag preserved in the result   |\n\n## 4. Data preparation pipeline\n\nDefined (not run). All steps run on a single `pandas.DataFrame`-equivalent view; in the stdlib-only degraded mode the same steps are implemented over `csv.DictReader` + dict-of-lists.\n\n1. **Load** from path supplied by the researcher; format inferred from extension (CSV assumed if unspecified).\n2. **Schema audit.** Per column: name, dtype category (`numeric | categorical | boolean | string | datetime`), `n_unique`, top-frequency value, missingness rate. Persist to `schema.json`. Reject columns that are constant or > 95 % missing — flag them rather than silently drop.\n3. **Type treatment.**\n   - `numeric` → optionally discretised into quantile bins `{5, 10, 20}`; raw values kept in a parallel numeric view for Mahalanobis / Isolation Forest.\n   - `categorical / boolean` → passthrough; `NaN` bucketed as `_NA`.\n   - `string` with `n_unique > 50` → top-K=20 + `_OTHER`; with `n_unique ≤ 50` → passthrough.\n   - `datetime` → derive {year-month, day-of-week, hour} features where present; recoded as ordered categoricals.\n4. **One-hot / item encoding** of the discretised matrix into a boolean item matrix `I ∈ {0,1}^{N×M}`.\n5. **Knowledge-leak-free split.** 70/30 train/test. When a target column exists, **stratified** by it. Indices saved to `splits.json`. **No fit-step ever sees the test rows.**\n6. **Rare-class subsampling knob (R3).** A function `downsample_rare_class(df, target, ratio, seed)` produces `{0.01, 0.05, 0.10, 0.20}` rare-class variants of train, leaving test untouched.\n7. **Configuration lock.** All knobs above (minsup grid, bins, seeds, ratios, caps) are serialised to a single `run_config.json` before any mining is invoked, so every reported number is reproducible.\n\n## 5. Baselines (three families)\n\nThe harness accepts miners through one protocol (see §5.4) — including any future Docta class — so adding a baseline is a 5-line wrapper.\n\n### 5.1 Pointwise anomaly scoring (per-row notion of rarity)\n\n- **Isolation Forest** on `(numeric ∪ one-hot categorical)` features after standard scaling; anomaly score = negative of the mean path length.\n- **Local Outlier Factor** (unsupervised) on the same view; `n_neighbors ∈ {20, 50}` swept.\n- **Mahalanobis distance** on numeric features after standard scaling; flag rows exceeding the χ² quantile at `α ∈ {0.001, 0.01}`. Numeric-only fallback when feature set is too degenerate.\n\n### 5.2 Itemset mining at very low minsup (R1, partial R2)\n\n- **Apriori**, `minsup ∈ {0.001, 0.005, 0.01}`, `max_itemset_size ∈ {2, 3, 4}`.\n- **FP-Growth** as a runtime cross-check on the same grids.\n- **Lift-filter + J-measure ranking** applied to the generated rules (per §3.2). Top-K by J-measure retained; K ∈ {50, 200, 1000}.\n- Confidence filter not used alone, but reported together with lift to expose trivial high-confidence rules with low lift.\n\n### 5.3 Contrast / emerging-pattern mining (R3 only, target-aware)\n\n- **Emerging-pattern growth**: enumerate itemsets in train, score by `growth_rate(X; c)` (§3.3) with smoothing, retain top-K (K ∈ {50, 200, 1000}) by growth rate subject to `support_c(X) ≥ min_class_sup`.\n- **Class-conditional lift** score (§3.3) reported alongside growth rate.\n- Optional **contrast-set miner** (STUCCO-style) when `n_classes ≥ 2` and class sizes permit.\n\n### 5.4 The protocol that catches any Docta class\n\n```text\nclass RarePatternMiner(Protocol):\n    name: str\n    family: Literal[\"anomaly\", \"itemset\", \"contrast\"]\n    def fit(self, item_matrix, config: RunConfig) -> \"RarePatternMiner\": ...\n    def mine(self) -> list[Pattern]:\n        # Pattern = NamedTuple(items: tuple[str], support, confidence|None,\n        #                     lift|None, leverage|None, j_measure|None,\n        #                     growth_rate|None, cell_metadata: dict)\n```\n\nAnti-fabrication note: this is *our* protocol, not a Docta API. If/when a Docta class is supplied, the wrapper translates its output to `list[Pattern]` — the harness then treats it identically to every other miner.\n\n## 6. Controls\n\n- **Random-feature null injection.** Append `K_null ∈ {3, 5}` synthetic columns drawn from a permuted copy of an existing column. A correct procedure should produce lift ≈ 1 and leverage ≈ 0 on those; **null-control rate = fraction of synthetic columns flagged as a \"rare pattern\" — expected ≈ 0**.\n- **Rare-class ratio sweep** `{1%, 5%, 10%, 20%}` for R3 sensitivity.\n- **Bin-count sweep** `{5, 10, 20}` to test sensitivity to discretisation.\n- **Itemset-size cap** `max_itemset_size ∈ {2, 3, 4}` keeps combinatorics bounded and is reported per cell.\n- **Knowledge-leak-free split** (§4 step 5). All fit/mine runs see train only.\n- **Seeds.** `seed ∈ {0, 1, 2, 3, 4}` set **before** any sampling and **before** any model fit; results aggregated as `mean ± std` across seeds.\n- **Runtime cap per cell** with a `TIMEOUT` flag preserved in the result table; timed-out cells are reported as \"—\" rather than dropped, so fragility is visible.\n- **Configuration lock.** `run_config.json` written once, hashed, and referenced in every reported number.\n\n## 7. Evaluation plan\n\nReported **per pattern family** (R1 / R2 / R3) **and per miner**, then aggregated across seeds.\n\n### 7.1 Pattern-level statistics\n\n- Number of itemsets / rules emitted.\n- Distributions (median, IQR, 95th–99th percentile) of: `support`, `confidence`, `lift`, `leverage`, `J-measure`, and for R3 `growth_rate`.\n\n### 7.2 Synthetic-injection ground truth (primary metric)\n\nOn a held-out synthetic dataset, plant **K_plant ∈ {10, 25}`** *known rare templates* (e.g. `A=high ∧ B=low`, `C=v1 ∧ D=v2 ∧ E∈{x,y}`) at controllable support `∈ [0.5%, 2%]`. Compare the miner's top-`k` returned patterns against the planted set.\n\n- **precision@k**, **recall@k**, **F1@k** for `k ∈ {10, 50, 200}`.\n- A returned pattern matches a planted template iff its item multiset equals the template's (after linting of feature names).\n\n### 7.3 Null-control rate\n\nFraction of injected random-feature columns flagged as rare-pattern hits; expected ≈ 0. Reported as the cleanest type-I check.\n\n### 7.4 Seed stability\n\nJaccard overlap of the **top-K** returned sets across the seed sweep, `K ∈ {20, 100}`. Reported per miner and per R-level.\n\n### 7.5 Train → test support drift\n\nFor each pattern emitted in train, recompute support / lift / confidence on test; report the median signed relative change. High drift flags overfitting to the train split.\n\n### 7.6 Rare-class recovery (R3)\n\nPR-AUC of (miner-ranked candidate patterns by `growth_rate`) against the rare-class labels on test, with the planted templates (§7.2) supplying positive labels in the synthetic track.\n\n### 7.7 Runtime\n\nWall-clock per pass, per cell of every sweep, plus the grand total. `TIMEOUT` cells are preserved with their flag.\n\n### 7.8 Reporting tables (schema only — no numbers)\n\n- `patterns_<miner>_<R_level>.md` — per-pattern statistics table.\n- `injection_<miner>.md` — precision/recall/F1@k at `k ∈ {10, 50, 200}`.\n- `stability_<miner>.md` — Jaccard@k across seeds.\n- `drift_<miner>.md` — train→test relative drift.\n- `runtime_<miner>.md` — per-cell wall-clock + total + `TIMEOUT` flag count.\n\n## 8. Feasibility under the bubblewrap sandbox\n\n| Layer                       | Status in this sandbox                                                                                                 |\n|-----------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Python 3.12 stdlib only     | Always runnable here: `csv`, `collections`, `itertools`, `statistics`, `math`, `random` cover schema audit, one-hot, support/lift/leverage/J-measure, null-control, seed aggregation. |\n| pandas / numpy / scipy      | Not preinstalled; **network is disabled**, so no `pip install` is possible inside the bubblewrap.                       |\n| scikit-learn (IForest, LOF) | Not preinstalled; same network constraint.                                                                            |\n| mlxtend (Apriori / FPGrowth)| Not preinstalled; same network constraint.                                                                            |\n| Docta                       | **Absent from `/workspace` and the entire visible filesystem.** `python3 -c \"import docta\"` raises `ModuleNotFoundError`. With network disabled, no install path is possible from inside the sandbox. |\n| GPU / CPUs                  | GPU off, 24 CPUs. Combinatorial blow-up from low-minsup Apriori/FP-Growth is bounded by the `max_itemset_size ∈ {2, 3, 4}` cap and the `minsup` floor; runtime cap per cell preserves failure visibility. |\n\n**Degraded mode.** When `pandas / numpy / sklearn / mlxtend` are unavailable, the design still runs R1 mining and §7.3 null-control on stdlib alone, drops §5.1 Isolation Forest / LOF (or replaces them with Mahalanobis-on-numeric-only), and skips Apriori/FP-Growth in favour of an in-place stdlib R1 counter.\n\n**Docta-agnostic posture.** Because no Docta source is supplied, the harness is parameterised by miner objects satisfying `RarePatternMiner` (§5.4). The pipeline is correct *whether or not* a Docta class is ever plugged in: a non-Docta miner simply fills the slot. No Docta class, function, or signature is named anywhere in this document, by design.\n\n## 9. Assumptions\n\nThese are the design-time defaults the researcher instructed us to lock rather than re-confirm.\n\n| # | Assumption / default                                                                                                                                  | Rationale / source                                                                                                  | Tag            |\n|---|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------|\n| 1 | Three-tier rare operationalisation (R1 / R2 / R3) per §3                                                                                              | Decomposes \"rare\" into the three independent meanings actually used in itemset, surprise, and contrast mining     | assumed        |\n| 2 | `minsup` sweep `{0.001, 0.005, 0.01}`                                                                                                                | Standard low-minsup grid; matches \"rare but not noise\" intuition                                                   | assumed        |\n| 3 | `max_itemset_size ∈ {2, 3, 4}`                                                                                                                        | Caps combinatorial explosion on 24 CPUs with no network access                                                    | assumed        |\n| 4 | `min_count = 5` rows                                                                                                                                  | Standard noise floor below which frequentist estimates are unstable                                               | assumed        |\n| 5 | Rare-class ratio sweep `{1%, 5%, 10%, 20%}`                                                                                                           | Probes R3 sensitivity to imbalance                                                                                  | assumed        |\n| 6 | Growth-rate thresholds `GR_min ∈ {2, 5, 10}`                                                                                                          | Standard emerging-pattern literature                                                                                | assumed        |\n| 7 | Quantile bins `{5, 10, 20}` for numeric columns                                                                                                       | Discrete-encode for itemset view; preserves rough monotonicity                                                   | assumed        |\n| 8 | High-cardinality strings (>`n_unique=50`): top-K=20 + `_OTHER`                                                                                        | Keeps the item matrix compact while retaining common signal                                                        | assumed        |\n| 9 | `lift_lo = 0.5`, `lift_hi = 1.5`; R2 ranked by `J-measure` with `leverage` then `lift` as tie-breakers                                                | Surprising-co-occurrence definition; matches Smyth & Goodman framing                                              | assumed        |\n|10 | 70/30 train/test, stratified by target when available                                                                                                 | Knowledge-leak-free, classical default                                                                              | assumed        |\n|11 | Seeds `{0, 1, 2, 3, 4}`; aggregated as `mean ± std`                                                                                                   | Five seeds balance stability reporting and step budget                                                             | assumed        |\n|12 | Runtime cap per cell with a `TIMEOUT` flag preserved in result tables                                                                                 | Keeps failure mode visible — never silently drop a slow cell                                                       | assumed        |\n|13 | Synthetic-injection ground truth with `K_plant ∈ {10, 25}` templates at `support ∈ [0.5%, 2%]`; metric `precision/recall/F1@k` at `k ∈ {10, 50, 200}` | The supplied natural data has no labelled rare-pattern ground truth                                                | assumed        |\n|14 | Pointwise anomaly baselines: Isolation Forest, LOF (`n_neighbors ∈ {20, 50}`), Mahalanobis (`α ∈ {0.001, 0.01}`)                                     | Three complementary per-row notions of rarity; Mahalanobis is the stdlib-only fallback                            | assumed        |\n|15 | Itemset baselines: Apriori + FP-Growth with the `minsup` and `max_itemset_size` grids                                                                 | Classical baselines; FP-Growth as runtime cross-check                                                              | assumed        |\n|16 | R3 contrast / emerging-pattern baselines: growth-rate + class-conditional lift, optional contrast-set miner                                           | Mirrors emerging-pattern literature; optional miner keeps the design feasible in degraded mode                    | assumed        |\n|17 | `null-control` expected ≈ 0; failures here are type-I indicators                                                                                      | Synthetic random columns must not look rare by construction                                                         | assumed        |\n|18 | Seed-stability measured by Jaccard overlap at `K ∈ {20, 100}`                                                                                         | Stability is a function of `K`; reporting two `K`s makes scale dependence explicit                                | assumed        |\n|19 | Train → test drift = median signed relative change of `support / lift / confidence`                                                                  | Robust to outliers and asymmetric                                                                                    | assumed        |\n|20 | `RarePatternMiner` protocol as the only integration surface; miner field types `(items, support, confidence, lift, leverage, j_measure, growth_rate)` | Lets any Docta class slot in without harness changes; the protocol itself is *our* interface, not a Docta API      | assumed        |\n|21 | Reporting format: one Markdown table per pattern family / metric group, no numerical claims                                                           | Matches the design-only contract; tables contain schemas, not numbers                                              | assumed        |\n\n## 10. Limitations (honest)\n\n- **No Docta in the supplied repository.** There is no Docta source, no docstring, no example notebook, no API to quote. The search `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname \"docta*\"` returns nothing, and `python3 -c \"import docta\"` raises `ModuleNotFoundError`. **`cannot be derived from supplied docs`** — the harness is therefore Docta-agnostic: it captures any class the researcher later supplies via the `RarePatternMiner` protocol, but no Docta class, function, or signature appears anywhere in this document by deliberate anti-fabrication discipline.\n- **No dataset in `/workspace`.** `task.json::environment.datasets = []` and no data files are present. The pipeline is defined for a path the researcher supplies at run time; nothing further can be checked at design time. **`cannot be derived from supplied docs`**.\n- **No execution is claimed.** No code was run beyond exploratory `ls`/`find`/`pip list`/`python -c \"import docta\"` probes. No mined patterns, no metrics, no runtime numbers are reported, by design.\n- **Ground truth only via synthetic injection.** Natural datasets carry no labelled \"rare-pattern\" ground truth; precision/recall/F1@k is only meaningful on the injected synthetic track. **Real-data performance is summarised only through pattern-level statistics, null-control rate, Jaccard stability, and train→test drift. **`assumed` (methodological, not a missing input)**.\n- **Network is disabled in the sandbox.** `pandas`, `numpy`, `scikit-learn`, `mlxtend` cannot be installed from inside the bubblewrap; the design therefore defines a stdlib-only degraded mode that covers R1 / null-control / metrics aggregation. **`cannot be derived from supplied docs`** (stated in §environment, not in `track.md`).\n- **Combinatorial fragility.** Even with `max_itemset_size ∈ {2, 3, 4}` and `minsup ≥ 0.001`, very wide / very tall feature matrices can still blow up. Runtime cap + `TIMEOUT` flag is the contractual answer; we do not pretend a guarantee. **`assumed` mitigation**.\n- **`track.md` is not a file.** Its only content is the one-line string inside `task.json::documents.track.md` — `\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"` — which this document interprets literally: design-only, no compute claimed. **`cannot be derived from supplied docs`** (no file body to inspect beyond that line).\n- **No memory metric.** `psutil` is not guaranteed in this stdlib-only sandbox; runtime is wall-clock only unless the researcher confirms `psutil` is acceptable. **`needs confirmation`** under the new policy, but recorded here as not-blocking.\n\n---\n\n*This document is a design artefact. It defines the experiment; it does not claim to have run it, does not report numerical results, and does not enumerate a Docta API. The harness assumes the role of integrators for whichever rare-pattern class — Docta or otherwise — the researcher eventually supplies.*\n", "evidence": "Inspection evidence (used to scope feasibility and limitations honestly):\n- `ls -la /workspace` → only `task.json` (363 bytes); no `track.md` file.\n- `cat /workspace/task.json` → only `documents.track.md = \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"` (the literal `track.md` content, quoted in §1 of initial inspection and reflected in §10 of this design).\n- `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname \"docta*\"` → no matches; `python3 -c \"import docta\"` → `ModuleNotFoundError`. Therefore no Docta class/function is named in this document.\n- `python3 -m pip list | grep -iE \"pandas|numpy|scikit|sklearn|scipy|mlxtend\"` → no matches; `network=\"disabled\"`; so no `pip install` and no Internet-fetched docs.\n- `task.json::environment` confirms `runtime=\"bubblewrap\"`, GPU off, 24 CPUs, `datasets=[]`, `software_environment=\"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\"`. These ground the Feasibility and Limitations sections.\n- All defaults (minsup, max_itemset_size, rare-class ratios, bins, top-K, split, seeds, k-grid, planted-template support) come from the researcher's approval message and are tabulated in §9.", "assumptions": [], "uncertainties": [], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-2349874f-c987-4c3d-8cf2-94359aa81601", "tool_call": {"id": "call-2349874f-c987-4c3d-8cf2-94359aa81601", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Deliver full rare-pattern experiment design\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Identifying Rare Patterns in Tabular Data with Docta \\u2014 Experiment Design (Design-Only)\\n\\n## 1. Title & TL;DR\\n\\n**Title.** A Docta-agnostic, plug-in experimental harness for surfacing rare patterns in tabular data, with three operational definitions of \\\"rare\\\" (R1 low marginal support, R2 rare-but-surprising co-occurrence, R3 rare-class-conditional), three baseline families (pointwise anomaly, low-minsup itemset, contrast/emerging pattern), and explicit synthetic-injection evaluation because no labelled rare-pattern ground truth is supplied.\\n\\n**TL;DR.** We design \\u2014 but do not execute \\u2014 an experiment that (i) operationalises \\\"rare pattern\\\" along three independent axes, (ii) ingests any tabular dataset and any \\\"miner\\\" object via a thin `RarePatternMiner` protocol so a future Docta class drops in without harness changes, (iii) compares the miner against stdlib-aware baselines under strict knowledge-leak-free controls and seed sweeps, and (iv) reports pattern-level statistics, synthetic-injection precision/recall/F1@k, null-control rate, seed stability (Jaccard on top-K), train-to-test support drift, rare-class PR-AUC, and runtime. Because the supplied repository contains no Docta artifacts and has no network, the design is Docta-agnostic: it captures whatever class the researcher later names instead of fabricating an API.\\n\\n## 2. Research question (restated)\\n\\n> \\\"How can you identify rare patterns in a tabular dataset using Docta?\\\"\\n\\nDecomposed into answerable design questions:\\n\\n- **Q1.** How is \\\"rare\\\" operationalised across the R1 / R2 / R3 axes, and which thresholds, formulas, and bounds apply?\\n- **Q2.** What data preparation is needed so that a generic rare-pattern procedure (including any Docta-provided one) can run reproducibly on a tabular input?\\n- **Q3.** Which baselines form a fair comparison group, and which metrics separate signal from noise on an *unlabelled* rare-pattern task?\\n- **Q4.** What controls and feasibility constraints govern the experiment (nulls, sweeps, leak-free splits, seeds, runtime, sandbox)?\\n\\n## 3. Operationalisation of \\\"rare\\\" \\u2014 three levels\\n\\nLet `X`, `Y` denote itemsets over the discretised/one-hot matrix; `N` the row count in the mining split (train); `c` a class label. All frequencies are estimated on the **training split only**.\\n\\n### 3.1 R1 \\u2014 Low marginal support\\n\\nA singleton or itemset whose absolute empirical support falls below a small floor and is therefore individually uncommon.\\n\\n- `support(X) = |{i : X \\u2286 row_i}| / N`\\n- Inclusion criterion: `support(X) \\u2264 minsup`  with `N \\u00b7 support(X) \\u2265 min_count` (\\u2265 5 rows by default) to suppress single-row noise.\\n\\n**Default `minsup` sweep:** `{0.001, 0.005, 0.01}` (\\u2248 0.1%, 0.5%, 1.0% of rows). For each `minsup`, R1 emits every qualifying itemset up to `max_itemset_size \\u2208 {2, 3, 4}`.\\n\\n### 3.2 R2 \\u2014 Rare-but-surprising co-occurrence\\n\\nItemsets whose joint frequency deviates strongly from independence, *and* whose absolute support remains small. We score every qualifying rule `X \\u21d2 y` (single consequent) using four classical quantities plus J-measure:\\n\\n- `confidence(X\\u2192y) = support(X \\u222a {y}) / support(X)`\\n- `lift(X\\u2192y) = support(X \\u222a {y}) / (support(X) \\u00b7 support({y}))`\\n- `leverage(X\\u2192y) = support(X \\u222a {y}) \\u2212 support(X) \\u00b7 support({y})`\\n- `J-measure(X; y) = P(X,y) \\u00b7 log2(P(X,y) / (P(X)\\u00b7P(y))) + P(X,\\u00acy) \\u00b7 log2(P(X,\\u00acy) / (P(X)\\u00b7P(\\u00acy)))`\\n\\n**Inclusion criterion (R2).** `support(X \\u222a {y}) \\u2264 minsup_R2` (use the same `minsup` sweep as R1 by default), **and** `(lift \\u2264 lift_lo  OR  lift \\u2265 lift_hi)` with default `lift_lo=0.5`, `lift_hi=1.5`, **and** the rule is ranked primarily by `J-measure` with `leverage` and `lift` as tie-breakers.\\n\\n### 3.3 R3 \\u2014 Rare-class-conditional / emerging patterns\\n\\nRestrict mining to the rare class bucket and look for itemsets that are over-represented there vs. the rest. The headline score is growth rate:\\n\\n- `growth_rate(X; c) = support_c(X) / support_\\u00acc(X)`            (with smoothing `support_c(X)+\\u03b5` / `support_\\u00acc(X)+\\u03b5`, \\u03b5 = `1/(2N)`)\\n- `support_diff(X; c) = support_c(X) \\u2212 support_\\u00acc(X)`\\n- `class_lift(X; c) = support_c(X) / [ P(c) \\u00b7 support(X) ]`     (factor by which `X` is enriched in class `c`)\\n\\n**Inclusion criterion (R3).** `support_c(X) \\u2265 min_class_sup` (\\u2265 5 rows in the rare class), `growth_rate(X; c) \\u2265 GR_min` (default `GR_min \\u2208 {2, 5, 10}`), reported per `max_itemset_size \\u2208 {2, 3, 4}`.\\n\\n**Rare-class ratio sweep:** `{1%, 5%, 10%, 20%}` of rows held by the rare class, induced by downsampling the rare class (or by class-stratified subsampling when the natural dataset has none). This sweep probes robustness of R3 to class imbalance.\\n\\n### 3.4 Defaults summary (locked for this design)\\n\\n| Knob                   | Default                                                  |\\n|------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|\\n| `minsup` sweep         | `{0.001, 0.005, 0.01}`                                   |\\n| `min_count`            | `5` rows                                                 |\\n| `max_itemset_size`     | `{2, 3, 4}`                                              |\\n| `lift` thresholds      | `lift_lo=0.5`, `lift_hi=1.5`                             |\\n| `J-measure`            | primary R2 ranking; `leverage`, `lift` as tie-breakers   |\\n| Rare-class ratio sweep | `{0.01, 0.05, 0.10, 0.20}`                               |\\n| Growth-rate threshold  | `GR_min \\u2208 {2, 5, 10}`                                    |\\n| Quantile bins          | `{5, 10, 20}`                                            |\\n| High-cardinality rule  | top-K=20 levels + `_OTHER` bucket                        |\\n| Split                  | 70/30 train/test, **stratified by target** when present  |\\n| Seeds                  | `{0, 1, 2, 3, 4}`                                        |\\n| Runtime cap per cell   | enforced with a `TIMEOUT` flag preserved in the result   |\\n\\n## 4. Data preparation pipeline\\n\\nDefined (not run). All steps run on a single `pandas.DataFrame`-equivalent view; in the stdlib-only degraded mode the same steps are implemented over `csv.DictReader` + dict-of-lists.\\n\\n1. **Load** from path supplied by the researcher; format inferred from extension (CSV assumed if unspecified).\\n2. **Schema audit.** Per column: name, dtype category (`numeric | categorical | boolean | string | datetime`), `n_unique`, top-frequency value, missingness rate. Persist to `schema.json`. Reject columns that are constant or > 95 % missing \\u2014 flag them rather than silently drop.\\n3. **Type treatment.**\\n   - `numeric` \\u2192 optionally discretised into quantile bins `{5, 10, 20}`; raw values kept in a parallel numeric view for Mahalanobis / Isolation Forest.\\n   - `categorical / boolean` \\u2192 passthrough; `NaN` bucketed as `_NA`.\\n   - `string` with `n_unique > 50` \\u2192 top-K=20 + `_OTHER`; with `n_unique \\u2264 50` \\u2192 passthrough.\\n   - `datetime` \\u2192 derive {year-month, day-of-week, hour} features where present; recoded as ordered categoricals.\\n4. **One-hot / item encoding** of the discretised matrix into a boolean item matrix `I \\u2208 {0,1}^{N\\u00d7M}`.\\n5. **Knowledge-leak-free split.** 70/30 train/test. When a target column exists, **stratified** by it. Indices saved to `splits.json`. **No fit-step ever sees the test rows.**\\n6. **Rare-class subsampling knob (R3).** A function `downsample_rare_class(df, target, ratio, seed)` produces `{0.01, 0.05, 0.10, 0.20}` rare-class variants of train, leaving test untouched.\\n7. **Configuration lock.** All knobs above (minsup grid, bins, seeds, ratios, caps) are serialised to a single `run_config.json` before any mining is invoked, so every reported number is reproducible.\\n\\n## 5. Baselines (three families)\\n\\nThe harness accepts miners through one protocol (see \\u00a75.4) \\u2014 including any future Docta class \\u2014 so adding a baseline is a 5-line wrapper.\\n\\n### 5.1 Pointwise anomaly scoring (per-row notion of rarity)\\n\\n- **Isolation Forest** on `(numeric \\u222a one-hot categorical)` features after standard scaling; anomaly score = negative of the mean path length.\\n- **Local Outlier Factor** (unsupervised) on the same view; `n_neighbors \\u2208 {20, 50}` swept.\\n- **Mahalanobis distance** on numeric features after standard scaling; flag rows exceeding the \\u03c7\\u00b2 quantile at `\\u03b1 \\u2208 {0.001, 0.01}`. Numeric-only fallback when feature set is too degenerate.\\n\\n### 5.2 Itemset mining at very low minsup (R1, partial R2)\\n\\n- **Apriori**, `minsup \\u2208 {0.001, 0.005, 0.01}`, `max_itemset_size \\u2208 {2, 3, 4}`.\\n- **FP-Growth** as a runtime cross-check on the same grids.\\n- **Lift-filter + J-measure ranking** applied to the generated rules (per \\u00a73.2). Top-K by J-measure retained; K \\u2208 {50, 200, 1000}.\\n- Confidence filter not used alone, but reported together with lift to expose trivial high-confidence rules with low lift.\\n\\n### 5.3 Contrast / emerging-pattern mining (R3 only, target-aware)\\n\\n- **Emerging-pattern growth**: enumerate itemsets in train, score by `growth_rate(X; c)` (\\u00a73.3) with smoothing, retain top-K (K \\u2208 {50, 200, 1000}) by growth rate subject to `support_c(X) \\u2265 min_class_sup`.\\n- **Class-conditional lift** score (\\u00a73.3) reported alongside growth rate.\\n- Optional **contrast-set miner** (STUCCO-style) when `n_classes \\u2265 2` and class sizes permit.\\n\\n### 5.4 The protocol that catches any Docta class\\n\\n```text\\nclass RarePatternMiner(Protocol):\\n    name: str\\n    family: Literal[\\\"anomaly\\\", \\\"itemset\\\", \\\"contrast\\\"]\\n    def fit(self, item_matrix, config: RunConfig) -> \\\"RarePatternMiner\\\": ...\\n    def mine(self) -> list[Pattern]:\\n        # Pattern = NamedTuple(items: tuple[str], support, confidence|None,\\n        #                     lift|None, leverage|None, j_measure|None,\\n        #                     growth_rate|None, cell_metadata: dict)\\n```\\n\\nAnti-fabrication note: this is *our* protocol, not a Docta API. If/when a Docta class is supplied, the wrapper translates its output to `list[Pattern]` \\u2014 the harness then treats it identically to every other miner.\\n\\n## 6. Controls\\n\\n- **Random-feature null injection.** Append `K_null \\u2208 {3, 5}` synthetic columns drawn from a permuted copy of an existing column. A correct procedure should produce lift \\u2248 1 and leverage \\u2248 0 on those; **null-control rate = fraction of synthetic columns flagged as a \\\"rare pattern\\\" \\u2014 expected \\u2248 0**.\\n- **Rare-class ratio sweep** `{1%, 5%, 10%, 20%}` for R3 sensitivity.\\n- **Bin-count sweep** `{5, 10, 20}` to test sensitivity to discretisation.\\n- **Itemset-size cap** `max_itemset_size \\u2208 {2, 3, 4}` keeps combinatorics bounded and is reported per cell.\\n- **Knowledge-leak-free split** (\\u00a74 step 5). All fit/mine runs see train only.\\n- **Seeds.** `seed \\u2208 {0, 1, 2, 3, 4}` set **before** any sampling and **before** any model fit; results aggregated as `mean \\u00b1 std` across seeds.\\n- **Runtime cap per cell** with a `TIMEOUT` flag preserved in the result table; timed-out cells are reported as \\\"\\u2014\\\" rather than dropped, so fragility is visible.\\n- **Configuration lock.** `run_config.json` written once, hashed, and referenced in every reported number.\\n\\n## 7. Evaluation plan\\n\\nReported **per pattern family** (R1 / R2 / R3) **and per miner**, then aggregated across seeds.\\n\\n### 7.1 Pattern-level statistics\\n\\n- Number of itemsets / rules emitted.\\n- Distributions (median, IQR, 95th\\u201399th percentile) of: `support`, `confidence`, `lift`, `leverage`, `J-measure`, and for R3 `growth_rate`.\\n\\n### 7.2 Synthetic-injection ground truth (primary metric)\\n\\nOn a held-out synthetic dataset, plant **K_plant \\u2208 {10, 25}`** *known rare templates* (e.g. `A=high \\u2227 B=low`, `C=v1 \\u2227 D=v2 \\u2227 E\\u2208{x,y}`) at controllable support `\\u2208 [0.5%, 2%]`. Compare the miner's top-`k` returned patterns against the planted set.\\n\\n- **precision@k**, **recall@k**, **F1@k** for `k \\u2208 {10, 50, 200}`.\\n- A returned pattern matches a planted template iff its item multiset equals the template's (after linting of feature names).\\n\\n### 7.3 Null-control rate\\n\\nFraction of injected random-feature columns flagged as rare-pattern hits; expected \\u2248 0. Reported as the cleanest type-I check.\\n\\n### 7.4 Seed stability\\n\\nJaccard overlap of the **top-K** returned sets across the seed sweep, `K \\u2208 {20, 100}`. Reported per miner and per R-level.\\n\\n### 7.5 Train \\u2192 test support drift\\n\\nFor each pattern emitted in train, recompute support / lift / confidence on test; report the median signed relative change. High drift flags overfitting to the train split.\\n\\n### 7.6 Rare-class recovery (R3)\\n\\nPR-AUC of (miner-ranked candidate patterns by `growth_rate`) against the rare-class labels on test, with the planted templates (\\u00a77.2) supplying positive labels in the synthetic track.\\n\\n### 7.7 Runtime\\n\\nWall-clock per pass, per cell of every sweep, plus the grand total. `TIMEOUT` cells are preserved with their flag.\\n\\n### 7.8 Reporting tables (schema only \\u2014 no numbers)\\n\\n- `patterns_<miner>_<R_level>.md` \\u2014 per-pattern statistics table.\\n- `injection_<miner>.md` \\u2014 precision/recall/F1@k at `k \\u2208 {10, 50, 200}`.\\n- `stability_<miner>.md` \\u2014 Jaccard@k across seeds.\\n- `drift_<miner>.md` \\u2014 train\\u2192test relative drift.\\n- `runtime_<miner>.md` \\u2014 per-cell wall-clock + total + `TIMEOUT` flag count.\\n\\n## 8. Feasibility under the bubblewrap sandbox\\n\\n| Layer                       | Status in this sandbox                                                                                                 |\\n|-----------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\\n| Python 3.12 stdlib only     | Always runnable here: `csv`, `collections`, `itertools`, `statistics`, `math`, `random` cover schema audit, one-hot, support/lift/leverage/J-measure, null-control, seed aggregation. |\\n| pandas / numpy / scipy      | Not preinstalled; **network is disabled**, so no `pip install` is possible inside the bubblewrap.                       |\\n| scikit-learn (IForest, LOF) | Not preinstalled; same network constraint.                                                                            |\\n| mlxtend (Apriori / FPGrowth)| Not preinstalled; same network constraint.                                                                            |\\n| Docta                       | **Absent from `/workspace` and the entire visible filesystem.** `python3 -c \\\"import docta\\\"` raises `ModuleNotFoundError`. With network disabled, no install path is possible from inside the sandbox. |\\n| GPU / CPUs                  | GPU off, 24 CPUs. Combinatorial blow-up from low-minsup Apriori/FP-Growth is bounded by the `max_itemset_size \\u2208 {2, 3, 4}` cap and the `minsup` floor; runtime cap per cell preserves failure visibility. |\\n\\n**Degraded mode.** When `pandas / numpy / sklearn / mlxtend` are unavailable, the design still runs R1 mining and \\u00a77.3 null-control on stdlib alone, drops \\u00a75.1 Isolation Forest / LOF (or replaces them with Mahalanobis-on-numeric-only), and skips Apriori/FP-Growth in favour of an in-place stdlib R1 counter.\\n\\n**Docta-agnostic posture.** Because no Docta source is supplied, the harness is parameterised by miner objects satisfying `RarePatternMiner` (\\u00a75.4). The pipeline is correct *whether or not* a Docta class is ever plugged in: a non-Docta miner simply fills the slot. No Docta class, function, or signature is named anywhere in this document, by design.\\n\\n## 9. Assumptions\\n\\nThese are the design-time defaults the researcher instructed us to lock rather than re-confirm.\\n\\n| # | Assumption / default                                                                                                                                  | Rationale / source                                                                                                  | Tag            |\\n|---|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------|\\n| 1 | Three-tier rare operationalisation (R1 / R2 / R3) per \\u00a73                                                                                              | Decomposes \\\"rare\\\" into the three independent meanings actually used in itemset, surprise, and contrast mining     | assumed        |\\n| 2 | `minsup` sweep `{0.001, 0.005, 0.01}`                                                                                                                | Standard low-minsup grid; matches \\\"rare but not noise\\\" intuition                                                   | assumed        |\\n| 3 | `max_itemset_size \\u2208 {2, 3, 4}`                                                                                                                        | Caps combinatorial explosion on 24 CPUs with no network access                                                    | assumed        |\\n| 4 | `min_count = 5` rows                                                                                                                                  | Standard noise floor below which frequentist estimates are unstable                                               | assumed        |\\n| 5 | Rare-class ratio sweep `{1%, 5%, 10%, 20%}`                                                                                                           | Probes R3 sensitivity to imbalance                                                                                  | assumed        |\\n| 6 | Growth-rate thresholds `GR_min \\u2208 {2, 5, 10}`                                                                                                          | Standard emerging-pattern literature                                                                                | assumed        |\\n| 7 | Quantile bins `{5, 10, 20}` for numeric columns                                                                                                       | Discrete-encode for itemset view; preserves rough monotonicity                                                   | assumed        |\\n| 8 | High-cardinality strings (>`n_unique=50`): top-K=20 + `_OTHER`                                                                                        | Keeps the item matrix compact while retaining common signal                                                        | assumed        |\\n| 9 | `lift_lo = 0.5`, `lift_hi = 1.5`; R2 ranked by `J-measure` with `leverage` then `lift` as tie-breakers                                                | Surprising-co-occurrence definition; matches Smyth & Goodman framing                                              | assumed        |\\n|10 | 70/30 train/test, stratified by target when available                                                                                                 | Knowledge-leak-free, classical default                                                                              | assumed        |\\n|11 | Seeds `{0, 1, 2, 3, 4}`; aggregated as `mean \\u00b1 std`                                                                                                   | Five seeds balance stability reporting and step budget                                                             | assumed        |\\n|12 | Runtime cap per cell with a `TIMEOUT` flag preserved in result tables                                                                                 | Keeps failure mode visible \\u2014 never silently drop a slow cell                                                       | assumed        |\\n|13 | Synthetic-injection ground truth with `K_plant \\u2208 {10, 25}` templates at `support \\u2208 [0.5%, 2%]`; metric `precision/recall/F1@k` at `k \\u2208 {10, 50, 200}` | The supplied natural data has no labelled rare-pattern ground truth                                                | assumed        |\\n|14 | Pointwise anomaly baselines: Isolation Forest, LOF (`n_neighbors \\u2208 {20, 50}`), Mahalanobis (`\\u03b1 \\u2208 {0.001, 0.01}`)                                     | Three complementary per-row notions of rarity; Mahalanobis is the stdlib-only fallback                            | assumed        |\\n|15 | Itemset baselines: Apriori + FP-Growth with the `minsup` and `max_itemset_size` grids                                                                 | Classical baselines; FP-Growth as runtime cross-check                                                              | assumed        |\\n|16 | R3 contrast / emerging-pattern baselines: growth-rate + class-conditional lift, optional contrast-set miner                                           | Mirrors emerging-pattern literature; optional miner keeps the design feasible in degraded mode                    | assumed        |\\n|17 | `null-control` expected \\u2248 0; failures here are type-I indicators                                                                                      | Synthetic random columns must not look rare by construction                                                         | assumed        |\\n|18 | Seed-stability measured by Jaccard overlap at `K \\u2208 {20, 100}`                                                                                         | Stability is a function of `K`; reporting two `K`s makes scale dependence explicit                                | assumed        |\\n|19 | Train \\u2192 test drift = median signed relative change of `support / lift / confidence`                                                                  | Robust to outliers and asymmetric                                                                                    | assumed        |\\n|20 | `RarePatternMiner` protocol as the only integration surface; miner field types `(items, support, confidence, lift, leverage, j_measure, growth_rate)` | Lets any Docta class slot in without harness changes; the protocol itself is *our* interface, not a Docta API      | assumed        |\\n|21 | Reporting format: one Markdown table per pattern family / metric group, no numerical claims                                                           | Matches the design-only contract; tables contain schemas, not numbers                                              | assumed        |\\n\\n## 10. Limitations (honest)\\n\\n- **No Docta in the supplied repository.** There is no Docta source, no docstring, no example notebook, no API to quote. The search `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname \\\"docta*\\\"` returns nothing, and `python3 -c \\\"import docta\\\"` raises `ModuleNotFoundError`. **`cannot be derived from supplied docs`** \\u2014 the harness is therefore Docta-agnostic: it captures any class the researcher later supplies via the `RarePatternMiner` protocol, but no Docta class, function, or signature appears anywhere in this document by deliberate anti-fabrication discipline.\\n- **No dataset in `/workspace`.** `task.json::environment.datasets = []` and no data files are present. The pipeline is defined for a path the researcher supplies at run time; nothing further can be checked at design time. **`cannot be derived from supplied docs`**.\\n- **No execution is claimed.** No code was run beyond exploratory `ls`/`find`/`pip list`/`python -c \\\"import docta\\\"` probes. No mined patterns, no metrics, no runtime numbers are reported, by design.\\n- **Ground truth only via synthetic injection.** Natural datasets carry no labelled \\\"rare-pattern\\\" ground truth; precision/recall/F1@k is only meaningful on the injected synthetic track. **Real-data performance is summarised only through pattern-level statistics, null-control rate, Jaccard stability, and train\\u2192test drift. **`assumed` (methodological, not a missing input)**.\\n- **Network is disabled in the sandbox.** `pandas`, `numpy`, `scikit-learn`, `mlxtend` cannot be installed from inside the bubblewrap; the design therefore defines a stdlib-only degraded mode that covers R1 / null-control / metrics aggregation. **`cannot be derived from supplied docs`** (stated in \\u00a7environment, not in `track.md`).\\n- **Combinatorial fragility.** Even with `max_itemset_size \\u2208 {2, 3, 4}` and `minsup \\u2265 0.001`, very wide / very tall feature matrices can still blow up. Runtime cap + `TIMEOUT` flag is the contractual answer; we do not pretend a guarantee. **`assumed` mitigation**.\\n- **`track.md` is not a file.** Its only content is the one-line string inside `task.json::documents.track.md` \\u2014 `\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"` \\u2014 which this document interprets literally: design-only, no compute claimed. **`cannot be derived from supplied docs`** (no file body to inspect beyond that line).\\n- **No memory metric.** `psutil` is not guaranteed in this stdlib-only sandbox; runtime is wall-clock only unless the researcher confirms `psutil` is acceptable. **`needs confirmation`** under the new policy, but recorded here as not-blocking.\\n\\n---\\n\\n*This document is a design artefact. It defines the experiment; it does not claim to have run it, does not report numerical results, and does not enumerate a Docta API. The harness assumes the role of integrators for whichever rare-pattern class \\u2014 Docta or otherwise \\u2014 the researcher eventually supplies.*\\n\", \"evidence\": \"Inspection evidence (used to scope feasibility and limitations honestly):\\n- `ls -la /workspace` \\u2192 only `task.json` (363 bytes); no `track.md` file.\\n- `cat /workspace/task.json` \\u2192 only `documents.track.md = \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"` (the literal `track.md` content, quoted in \\u00a71 of initial inspection and reflected in \\u00a710 of this design).\\n- `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname \\\"docta*\\\"` \\u2192 no matches; `python3 -c \\\"import docta\\\"` \\u2192 `ModuleNotFoundError`. Therefore no Docta class/function is named in this document.\\n- `python3 -m pip list | grep -iE \\\"pandas|numpy|scikit|sklearn|scipy|mlxtend\\\"` \\u2192 no matches; `network=\\\"disabled\\\"`; so no `pip install` and no Internet-fet